{{History of Balisca}}

The '''Cantonal Revolution''' ({{lang-bl|Revolución cantonal}}), also known as the '''Cantonal Rebellion''' ({{Lang-bl|Rebelión cantonal|links=no}}), was [[W:Cantonalism|cantonalist]] revolutionary insurrection that took place during the [[Anyos Federales|''Anyos Federales'']] period of [[History of Balisca|Baliscan history]], between August 1873 and May 1880. The conflict emerged from widespread opposition to the centralization of political and economic power under President [[Michelangelo Valentino]] and his successor [[Josèp Bernat]]. The revolution was ultimately defeated by federal forces, but the conflict resulted in significant political reforms, including the [[History of voting in Balisca|extension of suffrage]], limited labor rights, and the abolition of the provincial system in favor of [[Cantons of Balisca|cantons]]. 

The revolution originated in the ''[[Cosmocazo]]'' movement, a popular movement against the perceived extravagance and corruption of the Valentino administration and the [[Confederal government of Balisca|confederal government]] as a whole. The movement drew support from [[Trade unions in Balisca|labor unions]], [[Indigenous peoples of Balisca|indigenous communities]], progressive intellectuals, and regional political movements that opposed the concentration of power in the capital, [[Castejón]]. After nearly seven years of rebellions, the Cantonalists were defeated, but the cost of suppression and the ongoing threat of unrest compelled the federal government to implement reforms that partially addressed rebel grievances.

The defeat of the Cantonal Revolution was followed by the unofficial and clandestine [[1883 Confederal Convention]], an unsanctioned gathering of surviving Cantonalist leaders, labor organizers, and indigenous representatives who drafted a shadow constitution that would later serve as an ideological foundation for the [[Baliscan Revolution]].

==Background==
{{Main article|Anyos Federales|Cosmocazo}}

===Centralization under Valentino===

The [[Anyos Federales|Federal Years]] following the [[Baliscan Civil War]] had transformed Balisca from a war-torn confederation into an increasingly centralized [[W:Federation|federation]]. ''[[W:Caudillo|Caudillo]]'' [[Michelangelo Valentino]], who had served as the country's first president since 1838, oversaw a dramatic expansion of federal authority, using the powers granted to him during the civil war emergency to consolidate control under the guise of [[Anyos Federales#Reconstruction|directing reconstruction]]. This centralization program represented the solidification of the break with the decentralized traditions that had characterized Baliscan governance before the civil war, when substantial autonomy had been reserved to the Republics.

Valentino's administration pursued a program of systematic centralization across multiple domains that fundamentally altered the relationship between the federal government and the republics. Economically, the federal government assumed control over customs revenue, which had previously been shared with the provinces, and directed investment toward the capital region of Castejón and the [[Levante, Balisca|Levante]], at the expense of the interior. This concentration of resources in the capital created deep [[Western alienation|resentment in western territories]], which saw their wealth and resources being extracted to Castejón while receiving little in return. Within the [[Bayara|republic of Bayara]] there was still widespread resentment from the destruction wrought upon it during the [[Baliscan Civil War]], had historically maintained strong regional identities and traditions of self-governance, proved particularly resistant to this economic centralization.

Politically, Valentino and his cabinet built an extensive network of allies through the [[W:Spoils system|distribution of government positions and contracts]], creating a class of dependent elites whose fortunes were tied to the continuation of his administration. The president's patronage extended from high-level ministerial appointments to local postmasters, ensuring that a vast web of officials owed their positions to his favor. This system marginalized traditional provincial elites who had previously exercised significant influence in their republics, replacing them with men whose primary loyalty was to Valentino personally rather than to their local communities.

The military dimension of Valentino's centralization proved particularly contentious. The confederal army, which had grown to 250,000 men during the civil war, was retained as a permanent federal force rather than being demobilized or returned to provincial control as many had expected. This provided the president with the means to enforce the federal authority across the country, but it also represented a constant reminder of federal power in regions that had previously maintained their own military forces. Furthermore, the federal government claimed ownership of all [[Rail transport in Balisca|railways]] and [[W:Telegraph|telegraph]] lines, giving it control over both economic and information flows. This allowed the federal government to move troops rapidly to suppress dissent and to control the spread of news.

===''Cosmocazo''===
{{Main|Cosmocazo}}
[[File:1º república española La Flaca 19th century.jpg|left|thumb|Cartoon depicting political figures of the [[Anyos Federales|Federal Republic]], featuring [[Michelangelo Valentino]] in the middle sporting a {{W|phrygian cap}}.]]
By the early 1870s, opposition to increasing [[Centralism|centralization]] had coalesced into a broad political movement known as the ''Cosmocazo''. The term derived from the [[Baliscano]] word ''cosmo'', meaning "order" or "universe," combined with the augmentative suffix ''-azo'', suggesting a striking blow or transformative movement. The name reflected the belief among its adherents that Valentino's government represented a fundamental disruption of the natural order of Baliscan society, and that a dramatic reordering was necessary to restore proper balance.

The ''Cosmocazo'' movement encompassed several distinct political currents that found common cause in their opposition to federal centralization and criticism of increasing inequality. The Cantonalists, who would become the dominant faction, advocated for radical decentralization and sought to reorganize Balisca into autonomous cantons governed through direct democratic assemblies. 

Drawing inspiration from the federal tradition that had existed before the civil war, they argued that local communities should have maximum control over their own affairs, with the confederal government limited to matters of truly national concern such as foreign policy and defense. Labor activists, representing the growing [[Trade unions in Balisca|union movement]], opposed the federal government's pro-business policies and its suppression of worker organizing. They saw in the Cantonalist program an opportunity to establish labor-friendly policies at the local level even if such policies remained impossible at the federal level.

Indigenous movements also found common cause with the ''Cosmocazo''. [[W:Mapuche people|Wallche]] and [[W:Yaghan|Yamana]] leaders had grown increasingly frustrated with the encroachment of settlers on their traditional territories and the federal government's failure to honor treaties signed during earlier periods. Officials in [[Cerdanya]] and [[Bayara]] had accelerated the distribution of [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous lands]] to political allies and foreign investors, while the confederal government turned a blind eye to this. In one case, the confederal government deliberately "lost" a treaty with the [[Ñanderu people|Ñanderu tribe]], declared it invalid as a result, and opened up their ancestral land to settlement under the [[W:Homestead principle|homestead principle]]. 

Protracted court battles, ultimately resulting in limited success, prompted indigenous communities to seek allies among other opponents of the prevailing regime, and [[Progressivism in Balisca|progressive]] intellectuals, including writers, academics, and journalists, rounded out the coalition. They criticized the extravagance of the government, and called for political reform, using newspapers and pamphlets to spread their message despite increasing government censorship.

The movement gained particular traction after 1870, when the Valentino administration embarked on an ambitious program of public works in Castejón, developing much of today's [[Hisbanya]] borough. The construction of a presidential palace, the [[Plaza de Hesperides]], opera house, and grand boulevards was intended to demonstrate Balisca's industrial progress and modernity to foreign visitors and domestic audiences alike. However, critics denounced these projects as symbols of corruption and misplaced priorities in a country where many communities remained impoverished after the civil war. The contrast between the glittering new buildings of the capital and the poverty of the interior became a powerful rhetorical weapon for the Cosmocazo movement, which pointed to the extravagant spending as evidence that the federal government had lost touch with the needs of ordinary Baliscans.

===1873 constitutional crisis===
{{Main|1873 Baliscan constitutional crisis}}
The immediate trigger for the revolution was the Constitutional Crisis of 1873. In June of that year, the [[Confederalist Party]] supported by the [[National Democratic Party]] proposed a series of constitutional amendments that would fundamentally alter the balance of power in Balisca, shifting significant power into the hands of the federal government. The amendments would extend presidential terms from four to six years, eliminate term limits for the presidency, grant the president the power to dissolve provincial legislatures, and centralize legislative control over the judiciary. The proposed amendments were widely seen as an attempt to transform Balisca into an [[W:Oligarchy|oligarchy]], eliminating the last [[W:Crony capitalism|remaining checks corporate interests]].

When the [[Confederal Congress]] initially rejected the proposals, Valentino was pressured into dissolving the legislature by his corporate backers, and called for new elections under provisional rules that heavily favored his supporters. The new electoral regulations imposed property requirements for candidates, disqualified anyone who had held Cantonalist sympathies, and gave federal officials extensive powers to disqualify opposition voters. The move was denounced by opposition leaders as a constitutional coup, and even moderate politicians who had previously supported Valentino began to distance themselves from his administration.

The response was immediate and widespread: On 11 August 1873, workers in the city of [[Macaio]] seized the [[Cabildo of Macaio|Cabildo]] and declared the establishment of an [[Canton of Merced|autonomous canton]], refusing to recognize the authority of the dissolved Congress or the [[Valentino administration]]. The declaration, read from the balcony of the Cabildo by a coalition of local leaders led by the factory worker [[Marcos Aberasturi]], announced that Macaio would govern itself through a [[W:Popular assembly|popular assembly]] until legitimate federal authority was restored. Within days, similar uprisings occured in [[Alessandria]], [[Concepción]], [[Castejón]], [[Navassa]], and several other cities. The Cantonal Revolution had begun.

==Course of the rebellion==
{{See also|Timeline of the Cantonal Revolution}}

===Initial uprisings (August 1873 – December 1873)===

The first phase of the conflict was characterized by a series of coordinated uprisings across Balisca. The Cantonalist forces, though poorly equipped compared to the federal army, benefited from widespread popular support and intimate knowledge of local terrain. In many cases, federal garrisons found themselves surrounded by hostile populations and unable to move beyond the immediate vicinity of their barracks. The Cantonalists also benefited from the sympathy of many local officials, who quietly facilitated the rebellion by providing supplies, intelligence, and safe passage.

The initial Cantonalist declarations established the basic structure that would define the movement throughout the war: Each canton would be governed by a popular assembly with direct democratic decision-making, in which all adult residents (both men and women) could participate. These assemblies would elect administrative committees to handle day-to-day governance, but major decisions would be made by the full assembly. Cantons would coordinate through a federal council composed of delegates from each canton, but they would retain autonomy over local affairs, including taxation, education, and labor policy. The confederal army would be dissolved and replaced by local militias accountable to the cantonal assemblies.

Within three months, Cantonalist forces controlled approximately one-third of Baliscan territory, including much of Cerdanya, the [[Levante, Balisca|east coast]] outside of the urban areas, and significant portions of the [[Marañón basin]]. The rebellion proved particularly strong in areas with traditions of local autonomy, relative inaccessibility (such as the [[Marañón rainforest]]), or significant indigenous populations. The federal government, suprised by the scale and coordination of the uprising, struggled to mount an effective response as it found itself fighting simultaneous insurgencies across a vast territory.

===Federal mobilization and offense (1874)===

The federal government's initial response was hampered by divisions within the military. Many officers were sympathetic to the Cantonalist cause, having come from the same provincial regions that had risen in revolt. Several units defected to the rebellion, bringing their weapons and training with them. However, by early 1874, Valentino had consolidated control over the officer corps through a combination of promotions for loyal officers, purges of suspected Cantonalist sympathizers, and the recruitment of foreign mercenaries who had no local loyalties. The federal government also received substantial financial and material support from foreign investors, particularly from [[Zong]], [[Volisania]], [[Burawa]] and [[Mercia]], who feared that a Cantonalist victory would threaten their economic interests in Balisca.

The federal offensive of 1874 achieved significant successes. Using the railway network, which remained under federal control, federal forces rapidly moved troops between fronts, concentrating overwhelming force against individual cantons before Cantonalist forces could reinforce each other. The strategy proved devastatingly effective. Canton after canton fell to federal advances, and by mid-1874, the rebellion appeared to be on the verge of collapse as Cantonalist rebels found themselves increasingly isolated and outgunned.

The [[Siege of Castejón (1874)|federal siege against]] the [[Canton of Castejón]] from March to May 1874 became a symbol of the Cantonalist cause, due to the amount of bombardment carried out by federal troops. Observing the collapse of the authority of the state in it's own capital, the anarchist leader [[Lluís Ferrer]] commented that the conditions were ripe for transforming the lower-middle class [[W:Political revolution|political revolution]] into a [[W:Social revolution|social revolution]]. In Hesperia, internationalists led an  uprising in Alcoy, and in Estuaria, they rose up in [[Aigües dels Criolls]]. Internationalists led the cantonalist movement in the Bayaran cities of [[Sant Chuan]] and [[Palmarés]]. They also convinced sailors in [[Alessandria]] to join the uprising, and fought under the intransigents in the [[Alpujarra]]. 

However, the offensive also generated significant backlash. Reports of federal atrocities, including the execution of prisoners and the bombardment of civilian areas, spread throughout Balisca through underground networks of communication. These reports drove many previously neutral Baliscans into the Cantonalist camp. Indigenous communities, in particular, rallied to the Cantonalist cause after federal troops attacked several Wallche villages suspected of supporting the rebellion. What had begun as a political conflict increasingly took on the character of a war of national liberation, with the federal government seen as an occupying force in many regions.

===Stalemate and Valentino's Death (1875)===

By early 1875, the conflict had settled into a stalemate. Federal forces controlled the major cities and the railway network, but Cantonalist guerrillas dominated the countryside and the mountainous interior, and regularly blew up railways and bridges to hamper movement of federal troops. The federal army could occupy territory, but it could not pacify it; as soon as troops moved on, Cantonalist forces would reemerge. The rebellion had also developed more sophisticated organizational structures, with coordinated commands, supply networks, and even primitive manufacturing of weapons and ammunition in remote areas. The war had become a [[W:War of attrition|war of attrition]], and it was unclear which side could sustain the conflict longer.

On 15 March 1875, President Valentino died suddenly of a [[W:Heart attack|heart attack]] after a meeting with his cabinet, at age 7x. His death created a succession crisis, as he had not designated a clear heir and had systematically eliminated potential rivals within his administration. After several days of behind-the-scenes negotiations among military leaders, cabinet ministers, and representatives of the [[Volisan aristocracy|aristocracy]], [[Josèp Bernat]], the [[Secretariat of Defense (Balisca)|Minister of War]] and a close ally of Valentino, was appointed as the new president.

Bernat's ascension marked a turning point in the conflict. Unlike Valentino, who had sought to negotiate with the Cantonalists even while fighting them, Bernat was committed to the total destruction of the rebellion. A career military officer with little patience for political compromise, he viewed the Cantonalists as traitors who deserved no mercy. He immediately ordered an escalation of the war, authorizing the use of scorched-earth tactics and the summary execution of captured Cantonalist fighters. Bernat believed that only through force could the rebellion be defeated.

===Federal offensive, counter-insurgency and Cantonalist defeat (1876–1880)===

==Aftermath==
===Government reforms===
{{Main|1885 Baliscan constitutional reforms}}
Despite the federal government's military victory, the Cantonal Revolution had demonstrated the depth of popular discontent and the dangers of maintaining an exclusionary political system. The cost of suppressing the rebellion—both in lives and treasure—had been enormous, and the country remained deeply divided. Faced with the prospect of ongoing unrest and the economic disruption that would result, the Bernat administration reluctantly concluded that some concessions were necessary to restore stability.

The reforms announced between 1882 and 1885 represented a calculated effort to address the most pressing grievances while preserving the fundamental structure of federal authority. [[W:Universal male suffrage|Universal male suffrage]] was granted in 1883, eliminating the [[W:Property qualification|property requirements]] that had previously restricted the franchise to approximately 12 percent of the adult male population. However, the implementation of this reform was tempered by [[W:Literacy test|literacy requirements]] and residency qualifications that effectively disenfranchised many of the poorest and most mobile workers, particularly recent immigrants and indigenous people who did not speak [[Volisanian language|Volisanian]], [[Beron language|Beron]] or [[Baliscano]] as their first language.

The [[W:Right to unionize|right to unionize]] was recognized in 1884, but with significant limitations. Only craft unions were permitted; [[W:Industrial unions|industrial unions]] that organized all workers in a particular industry regardless of skill remained illegal. Unions were prohibited from engaging in political activity, endorsing candidates, or participating in electoral campaigns. The right to strike was severely restricted, with mandatory arbitration procedures that favored employers and prohibited work stoppages while disputes were being resolved. Despite these limitations, the reform represented a significant departure from the previous policy of suppressing all labor organization.

The most visible reform was the abolition of the [[Provinces of Balisca|provincial system]] in 1885 and its replacement with cantons. This change was presented as a victory for the Cantonalist vision of decentralized governance, but in practice, the new cantons were administrative units rather than autonomous polities. Cantonal governors were appointed by the federal president rather than elected locally, and cantonal assemblies were granted only advisory powers. Real authority remained concentrated in the federal government and its appointed officials. The reform satisfied no one completely: former Cantonalists saw it as a hollow mockery of their demands, while centralists resented even the symbolic concession to regional autonomy.

===The 1883 Confederal Convention===
{{Main|1883 Confederal Convention}}
[[File:Summit of Nations.png|thumb|''La Cimeira de les Naciones'' (the "Summit of Nations") by [[Xabier Sánchez]] depicts the [[1883 Confederal Convention]] and drafting of the proposed ''[[Cosmocazo]]''-era constitution.]]
While the federal government announced its reform program, surviving Cantonalist leaders and their allies organized an unofficial gathering that would become known as the 1883 Confederal Convention. Meeting in secret in a remote area of Bayara, approximately 200 delegates representing [[Trade unions in Balisca|labor unions]], [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|indigenous communities]], progressive political organizations, and former militants gathered to draft a proposal for Balisca's future.

The convention operated under conditions of extreme secrecy, with participants traveling by night and communicating through coded messages to avoid detection by federal authorities. The delegates were well aware that their gathering was illegal and that discovery would result in arrest, imprisonment, or execution. Among the participants were surviving militants that evaded capture, union organizers who had gone underground, indigenous leaders who had continued to resist [[W:Settler colonialism|settler encroachment]], and intellectuals. The Convention would later be nicknamed the "Summit of Nations", after the diverse makeup of the attendees, representing the full breadth of the Baliscan nation at the time.

Over three weeks of intense debate, the convention drafted a comprehensive constitution that embodied the Cantonalist vision; the document called for genuine cantonal autonomy with elected governors and assemblies possessing legislative authority, [[W:Direct democracy|direct democracy]] at the communal level through [[W:Popular assemblies|popular assemblies]], recognition of [[W:Indigenous sovereignty|indigenous self-governance over traditional territories]], full labor rights including the [[W:Right to strike|right to strike]] and to organize industrially, and a federal government strictly limited to [[International relations of Balisca|foreign affairs]], defense, and inter-cantonal coordination. The constitution was never implemented, but it was preserved and circulated secretly among opposition networks in the form of zines, before being published in full by [[La Solidaridad]] (an anarchist periodical) in 1905. The shadow constitution of 1883 would later be cited by revolutionaries in 1919 as the legitimate foundation for the post-revolutionary constitutional order, and many of its provisions would ultimately be [[Processo Revolucionario en Curso|implemented in the aftermath]].

===Political legacy===
The Cantonal Revolution, despite its military defeat, established important precedents that would shape Baliscan history in the decades that followed. The demonstration that a broad coalition of workers, indigenous peoples, and regional movements could mount a serious challenge to federal authority inspired future generations of activists and revolutionaries. The reforms granted in the war's aftermath, however limited, created openings for political organizing that had not previously existed. The labor movement, though constrained by the limitations on union activity, grew steadily in the decades after the war. The craft unions that were permitted under the 1884 reforms gradually expanded their membership and developed increasingly sophisticated organizational capacities. Indigenous communities, though denied the autonomy they had sought, maintained their distinct identities and continued to advocate for their rights.

[[W:Federalism|Federalism]] went into a decline in the wake of the Cantonalist uprising and the [[1885 Baliscan constitutional reforms|1885 constitutional reforms]]. Many within the federalist movement turned towards [[Anarchism in Balisca|anarchism]], which they saw as the logical conclusion of their desires for decentralisation, and federalism was later reconceived as a [[W:Precursors to anarchism|precursor to anarchism]]. Some anarchists, including [[Sebastià Mendizábal]], responded to the repression of the Cantonalist uprising by criticising the FRE-AIT's strategy of strike actions. [[Salvador Morago]] wrote about the need for "a long period of preparation and propaganda" before any revolutionary general strike could be carried out. [[Pablo Larrañaga]] characterised the uprising as a wholly [[W:Political revolution|political revolution]] and criticised it for its middle-class character, which he believed to set the working-class back. On the other hand, [[Yelena Calluqueo]] identified the Cantonalist revolt with the anarchist conception of the [[W:Autonomy|autonomous]] [[W:Municipality|municipality]]. In his own analysis of the cantonalist revolt, Friedrich Engels criticised the anarchists themselves for failing to take a leading role in the movement. He denounced the FRE for its [[W:Apoliticism|apoliticism]], its refusal to support the Pi i Margall government and its alliance with the intransigent republicans. The Cantonal Revolution also alienated many other republicans from the model of federalism, [[W:Direct democracy|direct democracy]] and political autonomy. Instead they began to advocate for a [[W:Centralised government|centralised government]], which would be elected according to [[W:Representative democracy|representative democracy]] and would make political decisions through parliament.

The federal government's victory had been achieved through overwhelming force rather than political reconciliation, and the underlying grievances that had sparked the rebellion remained largely unaddressed. The [[Baliscan Gilded Age|Gilded Age]] that followed would see these tensions intensify rather than dissipate, setting the stage for the revolutionary upheaval that would ultimately grip Balisca in the 20th century.

==In popular culture==
{{Main article|Cantonal Revolution in popular culture}}

The Cantonal Revolution has been the subject of numerous works of literature, film, and art in Balisca. ''La guerra de los cantones'' (1892), a novel by [[Mariano Azuela]], follows a family caught between federal and Cantonalist forces, exploring the human cost of the conflict and the difficult moral choices faced by ordinary people. ''Cantonalistas'' (1956), a film by [[Luis Buñuel]], depicts the siege of Daramazán and its aftermath, using the destruction of the city as a metaphor for the broader costs of war. ''The Shadow Constitution'' (1978), a documentary produced by [[Castejón Broadcasting Collective]] (CRC), examined the 1883 Confederal Convention and its role in inspiring the later revolution.

